<h2>What makes Section 19 distinctive</h2>
<p>The Family Courts Act replaced the scattered appellate provisions of the personal-law statutes with one consolidated route. Three features matter in practice. First, the appeal lies on both facts and law, so the High Court can re-appreciate evidence — findings on cruelty, desertion or the child&rsquo;s welfare are genuinely open for review. Second, the appeal is heard by a Bench of two judges, which is why matrimonial appeals in Delhi are listed before a Division Bench. Third, the limitation period is thirty days, a deliberately tight timeline that reflects the statute&rsquo;s concern that family disputes not linger.</p>

<h2>The interlocutory line</h2>
<p>The exclusion of interlocutory orders generates the most frequent threshold fights. An order framing issues, adjourning proceedings or summoning a witness is plainly interlocutory. Orders with more bite — interim custody arrangements, interim maintenance under the matrimonial statutes, injunctions over property — occupy contested ground, and the maintainability answer can differ with how decisively the order affects rights. Where the appeal door is shut, parties sometimes invoke the High Court&rsquo;s supervisory jurisdiction, but that path is narrow: supervisory review corrects jurisdictional error and perversity, and does not reweigh evidence.</p>

<h2>Timing traps</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>The thirty-day clock.</strong> Obtain the certified copy immediately; the short period leaves little room for drift, and condonation is a plea for indulgence, not a right.</p>
  <p><strong>Remarriage.</strong> The safe course after a divorce decree is to wait until the appeal period has expired without an appeal, or until an appeal has been finally decided. Marrying within the window invites complications no one wants to litigate.</p>
  <p><strong>Cross-objections.</strong> A respondent partly aggrieved — say, by the quantum of permanent alimony in a decree otherwise in their favour — should consider their own challenge rather than assume the issue can be reopened in reply.</p>
</div>

<h2>How the appeal is actually heard</h2>
<p>A Section 19 appeal in Delhi proceeds on the Family Court&rsquo;s record: the pleadings, the evidence recorded, and the judgment. Fresh evidence is exceptional and needs a specific application. The Division Bench typically explores settlement and mediation at the outset — matrimonial appeals are among the matters most frequently referred to the Delhi High Court Mediation and Conciliation Centre — and interim arrangements on maintenance, custody access and travel can be sought pending the appeal. The appellate court&rsquo;s powers are plenary: it can affirm, reverse or modify the decree, including recalibrating alimony and custody terms to the date of its own judgment.</p>

<h2>The practical sequence for an intending appellant</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Day 0.</strong> Decree pronounced. Apply for the certified copy the same day.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Week 1&ndash;2.</strong> Brief appellate counsel; settle grounds attacking specific findings of fact and law; compile the trial record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Within 30 days.</strong> File the appeal with any interim applications — stay of the decree&rsquo;s operation, interim maintenance, custody arrangements.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>First listing.</strong> Expect notice, a mediation reference where feasible, and interim directions holding the position until the appeal is decided.</div>
</div>
<p>An appeal is not a retrial, but under Section 19 it is the closest thing family litigation offers: a full second look, by two judges, at both the facts and the law. Treating the thirty days after the decree with the urgency they demand is what keeps that look available.</p>
